## Context

Fable's post-merge review of PR #370 measured that `erg sync --upstream`
runs `git fetch --depth=1` inside the adopter repository. Git consequently
leaves a durable `.git/shallow` graft there. The test proves that old upstream
history is absent but cannot see this cost.

The same review found that a missing blob at the trusted project origin now
fails a chain that is intended to remain offline-tolerant.

Review: https://github.com/MinhHaDuong/git-erg/pull/370#pullrequestreview-5233295500

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `sync --upstream` fetches shallowly in a throwaway repository confined
      to the ticket store and leaves the adopter repository non-shallow without
      importing upstream history
- [ ] focused integration coverage proves both halves of that property
- [ ] a reachable trusted project origin missing its store-relative binary
      warns but exits 0; explicit custom/upstream sources still fail hard
- [ ] help text describes where each fetch runs
- [ ] `make test`, `make validate`, and the ticket DAG pass
- [ ] external verification has no unresolved findings

## Review disposition

Fable also suggested hand-adding the predecessor `.ergrc` hash. That is not
part of this fix: 0281 already measured zero beneficiaries, closed the table
as wontfix, and records that it must never be hand-typed because a false hash
can silently clobber a local edit. The existing preserve + exit 2 degradation
is deliberately safer until empirical need justifies the generated-history
design specified there.
